1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,440
You mount a network share by hand, and then it is gone after a reboot,

2
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:06,064
or you forget to unmount it.

3
00:00:06,084 --> 00:00:10,065
What if the share just mounted itself, only when you open the folder?

4
00:00:10,535 --> 00:00:12,926
In this video: configure auto F S.

5
00:00:17,446 --> 00:00:20,029
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:20,499 --> 00:00:22,015
Configure autofs.

7
00:00:22,485 --> 00:00:25,163
We'll write two small files, start the service,

8
00:00:25,183 --> 00:00:28,665
and watch a share mount itself the moment we open the folder,

9
00:00:28,685 --> 00:00:31,019
and unmount itself when we stop using it.

10
00:00:39,539 --> 00:00:44,163
Auto F S mounts a file system on demand. It does not mount at boot.

11
00:00:44,183 --> 00:00:49,083
It waits. The first time anything opens the folder, auto F S mounts it.

12
00:00:49,103 --> 00:00:53,450
After a while with nobody using it, auto F S unmounts it again.

13
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,178
It needs two files.

14
00:00:59,198 --> 00:01:04,765
The master map says which folder auto F S watches, and which map file describes it.

15
00:01:04,785 --> 00:01:08,198
The map file says what to mount inside that folder:

16
00:01:08,218 --> 00:01:11,361
a name, the options, and the server colon path.

17
00:01:16,831 --> 00:01:21,835
Before the demo, silently, the lab installs the packages and sets up an N F S

18
00:01:21,855 --> 00:01:24,837
server with one shared folder. One lab detail:

19
00:01:24,857 --> 00:01:27,121
if the server is this same machine,

20
00:01:27,141 --> 00:01:31,036
auto F S shortcuts with a local bind mount instead of N F S.

21
00:01:31,056 --> 00:01:35,995
So our lab server is the address one twenty seven dot zero dot zero dot two,

22
00:01:36,015 --> 00:01:41,280
a second address of this machine. On the exam, you will use the real server name.

23
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,409
The master map is a file ending in dot auto F S,

24
00:01:55,429 --> 00:01:58,963
inside slash E T C slash auto dot master dot D.

25
00:01:58,983 --> 00:02:04,028
Each line has the folder to watch, the map file, and options. Here:

26
00:02:04,048 --> 00:02:09,321
slash M N T slash auto, the map file slash E T C slash auto dot N F S,

27
00:02:09,341 --> 00:02:11,513
and a timeout of ten seconds.

28
00:02:15,983 --> 00:02:20,233
To write the file, echo prints the line. The pipe symbol hands it to tee.

29
00:02:20,253 --> 00:02:24,795
Sudo tee writes it into the file with root rights, and shows us what it wrote.

30
00:02:31,315 --> 00:02:37,474
The map file has one line: a name, data, then the options, then server colon path.

31
00:02:37,494 --> 00:02:42,900
Dash fstype equals N F S four is the type, and R W means read and write.

32
00:02:47,370 --> 00:02:51,616
The name, data, becomes a folder inside slash M N T slash auto.

33
00:02:51,636 --> 00:02:53,580
We never create it ourselves.

34
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:02,648
Now we start the service. Systemctl enable dash dash now auto F S.

35
00:03:02,668 --> 00:03:06,869
Enable starts it at every boot, and now starts it right away.

36
00:03:11,339 --> 00:03:13,894
Ls on the watched folder prints nothing,

37
00:03:13,914 --> 00:03:18,658
and findmnt on the data folder prints nothing too. Nothing is mounted yet.

38
00:03:25,178 --> 00:03:27,062
Now we just look inside.

39
00:03:27,082 --> 00:03:32,376
Ls on slash M N T slash auto slash data, and hello dot txt appears.

40
00:03:32,396 --> 00:03:33,883
We never ran mount.

41
00:03:38,353 --> 00:03:42,819
Cat prints the file from the server. And findmnt shows what happened:

42
00:03:42,839 --> 00:03:44,574
auto F S mounted it for us.

43
00:03:44,594 --> 00:03:49,320
The type is N F S four, and the source is the server and the shared path.

44
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:00,157
Now we leave it alone.

45
00:04:00,177 --> 00:04:04,409
Sleep twenty waits twenty seconds, longer than our ten second timeout.

46
00:04:08,879 --> 00:04:12,663
Findmnt prints nothing. Auto F S unmounted it by itself.

47
00:04:12,683 --> 00:04:15,515
Touch the folder again, and it comes back.

48
00:04:22,035 --> 00:04:25,471
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

49
00:04:26,991 --> 00:04:31,944
We reboot. Auto F S is still enabled, so nothing needs mounting by hand.

50
00:04:31,964 --> 00:04:35,605
Findmnt shows nothing, because nobody has looked yet.

51
00:04:35,625 --> 00:04:40,233
Then cat reads the file, and findmnt shows the share mounted again.

52
00:04:40,253 --> 00:04:44,102
This is why we use auto F S instead of mounting by hand.

53
00:04:57,622 --> 00:05:04,925
Your turn. A server at one twenty seven dot zero dot zero dot two shares slash S R V slash exam.

54
00:05:04,945 --> 00:05:10,799
Configure auto F S so that slash remote slash exam mounts it with N F S four,

55
00:05:10,819 --> 00:05:15,987
and hello dot txt can be read. Auto F S must be enabled and running.

56
00:05:16,007 --> 00:05:17,207
Run the checker.

57
00:05:24,677 --> 00:05:26,428
Try before you open the solution.

58
00:05:31,948 --> 00:05:34,979
Next up: extend existing logical volumes.

59
00:05:35,449 --> 00:05:38,351
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
